From: Artem Pelenitsyn <a.pelenitsyn@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 20:17:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] Compile with the ghc-compat that mhs has installed

ghc-compat holds modules that are part of base in GHC but not in mhs, such
as GHC.Stack, GHC.Exts and Language.Haskell.TH.Syntax. Packages written for
GHC import them while depending only on base, so MicroCabal adds ghc-compat
to the dependencies of every package but base, ghc-compat and the packages
of MicroHs itself.

Do likewise in Distribution.Simple.MHS, without the solver knowing: if mhs
has ghc-compat installed in its own package directory, as
'mcabal install ghc-compat' does, preload it for every component, unless its
package is one of those or it depends on a ghc-compat itself. See
Note [MicroHs and ghc-compat], which also says what this means for the
store.

Measured on the 91 packages of MicroHs's Makefile.packages, the list its
hackage-ci workflow installs with mcabal, with ghc-compat installed with
mcabal: 80 build, up from 62. Of the other 11, 5 fail because mhs's hashable
is presented at version 1.0.0.0, MicroCabal's number, while
unordered-containers requires hashable >= 1.4, so the solver builds hashable
from Hackage, which mhs cannot compile; 4 because of upper bounds that mcabal
ignores (the indexed-traversable fork caps containers below 0.8, and
QuickCheck from git is 2.19.0.0, which hspec-core excludes); xhtml needs a
module that mhs's base lacks, and terminfo the curses C library.

